From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Davis Rollman <drollman@meta.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 13:20:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] shrink retained bytecode side tables

Summary:
Every compiled function keeps a few side tables alive for as long as it exists: the per-instruction spans used for error messages, the statement-location table, and the local names. Two things made them bigger than they needed to be. The tables were built by pushing into `Vec`s and never trimmed, so they carried doubling slack. And every instruction's slow arg embedded a `Vec<FrameSpan>` of "extra" spans that only `DictNPop` ever populates (one per key, so a duplicate or unhashable key error can point at the key); for every other instruction it was 24 bytes of empty vector header.

This diff drops that field. Dictionary key spans now live in a single `dictnpop_spans` array on the function's `End` instruction, and each `DictNPop` carries a `u32` offset into it. The writer appends a dictionary's key spans when it emits the instruction, which happens after any nested dictionaries have been compiled, so nested literals get their own disjoint ranges. On the error path the instruction already knows it is the faulting one, so it walks to `End` (the same walk the existing span lookup does) and indexes the array. `finish` also shrinks the slow-arg and statement-location vectors before they're retained.

This is the layout jtbraun suggested in review. `DictNPop` grows by 8 bytes for the offset, visible in the updated golden; what it replaces was 24 bytes per instruction plus a heap allocation per dictionary literal. Against the parent diff, retained jemalloc Allocated drops 3.8% on a floor daemon and 1.6% on adfinder, with CPU flat (test plan).

Reviewed By: jtbraun

Differential Revision: D116723107

